Governance Ecosystem

Foundation: The Arbitrum Foundation

· Role: Cayman Islands foundation company; tasked with developing and nurturing Arbitrum ecosystem; operates as neutral steward for ArbitrumDAO
· Status: Active
· Sources: https://docs.arbitrum.foundation/what-foundation

Foundation: The Arbitrum OpCo Foundation

· Role: Operational mesh layer for ArbitrumDAO; manages DAO-approved initiatives; funded 30M ARB for 2.5 years
· Status: Active
· Sources: https://docs.arbitrum.foundation/what-opco

DAO: ArbitrumDAO

· Role: Community-driven governance mechanism; ARB token holders propose and vote on changes
· Status: Active
· Sources: https://docs.arbitrum.foundation

Council: Security Council

· Role: 12-member board elected by token holders every six months; authorized to intervene in emergencies
· Status: Active
· Sources: https://coinmarketcap.com

Committee: Oversight and Transparency Committee (OAT)

· Role: 5 members elected yearly; holds OpCo Foundation accountable; ratifies budget; ensures transparency
· Members: A.J. Warner, Frisson, Gavin Wang, Patrick McCorry, Pedro Breuer
· Status: Active
· Sources: https://docs.arbitrum.foundation/what-opco

Validator Group: Tidak diketahui

---

Ecosystem Risks

Risk: Centralized Sequencer Dependency

· Description: Arbitrum's centralized sequencer introduces a critical dependency. Although mechanisms like the Delayed Inbox allow users to bypass the sequencer, few projects rely on these paths. Research has shown that the centralized sequencer creates opportunities for latency-sensitive strategies.
· Source: https://cantina.xyz; https://arxiv.org

Risk: Security Council Emergency Powers

· Description: The Security Council, a 12-member elected board, can intervene in emergencies to freeze funds. This power raises concerns about whether such authority undermines true decentralization. In April 2026, the Security Council froze over 30,000 ETH (~$71 million) tied to the KelpDAO exploit.
· Source: https://coinmarketcap.com; https://www.coindesk.com

Risk: Bridge Dependency

· Description: Reliance on third-party bridging providers for asset transfers outside the Arbitrum ecosystem introduces counterparty and security risks.
· Source: https://docs.arbitrum.io/launch-arbitrum-chain/integrations/infrastructure-providers

